#{[#edb/ref 17592186050929 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065114} :activity/id #uuid "8829b364-1582-5e1a-894c-d8837d69b705" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065444 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we introduce a method of integration that can be used in cases where integration by substitution fails. The method is known as **integration by parts**. The so-called **by-parts formula** is \n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis method is handy for integrating products of functions where one factor can be easily integrated, and the other factor can easily be differentiated. \n\nLet's see how to apply it to evaluate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nThe idea is to call one of the factors $u,$ the other factor $v',$ and then apply the by-parts formula. We'll choose the following:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{\\color{blue}x}_{\\large\\color{blue}u}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{red}e^{2x}}_{\\large\\color{red}v'} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, if ${\\color{blue}u} = {\\color{blue}x}$ then we differentiate to get ${\\color{blue}u'} = {\\color{blue}1}.$ And if ${\\color{red}v'} = {\\color{red}e^{2x}}$ then we integrate to get ${\\color{red}v} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}}.$ Note that we don't bother with the constant of integration here.\n\nWe now have everything we need, so we can substitute into the \"by-parts\" formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {{x\\cdot {e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{u{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={{x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{uv}-\\int {{\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 1}_{⏟}}_{v{u}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}(2x-1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! Notice that integration by parts allows us to take a complicated integral $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$ and replace it with the simpler $\\displaystyle \\int e^{2x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$ plus some additional terms." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065115}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064099 :question/id #uuid "60a223c7-3d1c-50d6-be7c-330ba039635c" :question/math-academy-id "e-491" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv' \\, \\textrm{d}x= uv - \\int vu' \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle  \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle  \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{7x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}-\\int \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}\\int {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}(\\frac{{e}^{7x}}{7})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{49}{e}^{7x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Indefinite Integral Containing an Exponential Expression"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065116}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064100 :question/id #uuid "804f45cd-2082-5521-b4db-a32169716626" :question/math-academy-id "e-492" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x \\cos{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x \\cos{2x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Indefinite Integral Containing a Trigonometric Expression"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065160}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065445 :tutorial/content "If you thought the by-parts formula looked similar to the product rule for differentiation, then you'd be right. In fact, that's precisely where it comes from!\n\nTo work out the formula, we recall the product rule for differentiation,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)=u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}+ v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x},\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ and $v$ are functions of $x.$ \n\nIf we rearrange this formula a bit, we get\n\n$$\nu\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)- v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's now integrate both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)\\,\\textrm{d}x- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nUsing the fundamental theorem of calculus, we can use the fact that $\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(uv)\\,\\textrm{d}x=uv,$ and we arrive at the by-parts formula:\n\n$$\n\\int u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$" :tutorial/title "Deriving the By Parts Formula From the Product Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065446 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our first example,\n\n$$\n\\int xe^{2x} \\textrm{d}x\\,.\n$$\n\nNote that when we selected our $u$ and $v',$ we could have picked them the other way around. So instead, we could have said\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={e}^{2x}\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the by-parts formula using this selection, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{2x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}-\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, while the above is a mathematically correct statement, the integral on the right-hand side is *more* complicated than the integral we started with. Ouch!\n\nTherefore, it's crucial that we select our $u$ and $v'$ in such a way that the problem is simplified, not made more complicated." :tutorial/title "Final Remark"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064117 :question/id #uuid "aaeb0407-771b-5dbc-8a50-e7bc0e3477c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6209" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/5} x\\sin(5x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\int (-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+{\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5})|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)]+[\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\pi }{25}\\cdot (-1)+0]+[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Definite Integral Using Integration by Parts"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065117}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Integration by Parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064413}] [#edb/ref 17592186050407 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101678} :activity/id #uuid "d19bc789-6fc7-5102-bc7b-8f270f9b39a8" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101959 :tutorial/content "When multiplying two matrices in general, we apply the same approach as usual:\n\n- First, we verify that the two matrices are conformable for multiplication.\n\n- Then, we multiply each row in the first matrix by each column in the second.\n\nFor example, let's find $AB,$ where \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we verify the conformability for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{3}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\cdot} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{3}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{B} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{C}\n$$\n\nSo the matrices are conformable for multiplication, and the product is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let's set up the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        -2 & 2 & 1 \\\n\t1 & -1 & 3 \n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack}_{A}\n\n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        -5 & 2  \\\n\t2 & -2  \\\n\t-4 & 1 \n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack}_{B}\n\n\t&= \n\t\\underbrace{\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        \\ast & \\ast  \\\n        \\ast & \\ast \n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\t}_{C}\n}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the entries of the product matrix (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $c_{11},$ the entry at the intersection of the $1$st row and $1$st column, we calculate the dot product of the $1$st row of $A$ and the $1$st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        \\color{blue}-2 & \\color{blue}2 & \\color{blue}1 \\\n\t\\color{lightgray}1 & \\color{lightgray}-1 & \\color{lightgray}3 \n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n         \\color{red}-5 & \\color{lightgray}2  \\\n\t\\color{red}2 & \\color{lightgray}-2  \\\n\t\\color{red}-4 & \\color{lightgray}1\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n%\t&= \n%    \\left\\lbrack\n%   \\matrix {\n%       ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}}) & \\ast  \\\n%        \\ast & \\ast\n%    }\n%    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{10} & \\ast  \\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{10} = ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}})\n}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{12},$ we calculate the dot product of the $1$st row of $A$ and the $2$nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        \\color{blue}-2 & \\color{blue}2 & \\color{blue}1 \\\n\t\\color{lightgray}1 & \\color{lightgray}-1 & \\color{lightgray}3 \n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        \\color{lightgray}-5 & \\color{red}2  \\\n\t\\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{red}-2  \\\n\t\\color{lightgray}-4 & \\color{red}1\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n%\t&= \n%    \\left\\lbrack\n%    \\matrix {\n%        10 & ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}  \\\n%        \\ast & \\ast\n%    }\n%    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        10 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-7}  \\\n        \\ast & \\ast\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-7} = ({\\color{blue}{-2}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n}\n$$\n\nTo find $c_{21},$ we calculate the dot product of the $2$nd row of $A$ and the $1$st column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        \\color{lightgray}-2 & \\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{lightgray}1 \\\n\t\\color{blue}1 & \\color{blue}-1 & \\color{blue}3 \n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n         \\color{red}-5 & \\color{lightgray}2  \\\n\t\\color{red}2 & \\color{lightgray}-2  \\\n\t\\color{red}-4 & \\color{lightgray}1\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n%\t&= \n%    \\left\\lbrack\n%    \\matrix {\n%        10 & -7  \\\n%        {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}}) & \\ast\n%    }\n%    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        10 & -7  \\\n        \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-19} & \\ast\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{-19} = {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-5}}) + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-4}})\n}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find $c_{22},$ we calculate the dot product of the $2$nd row of $A$ and the $2$nd column of $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        \\color{lightgray}-2 & \\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{lightgray}1 \\\n\t\\color{blue}1 & \\color{blue}-1 & \\color{blue}3 \n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        \\color{lightgray}-5 & \\color{red}2  \\\n\t\\color{lightgray}2 & \\color{red}-2  \\\n\t\\color{lightgray}-4 & \\color{red}1\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n%\t&= \n%    \\left\\lbrack\n%    \\matrix {\n%        10 & -7  \\\n%        -19 & {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n%    }\n%    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t= \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        10 & -7  \\\n        -19 & \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{7}\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack, && \\quad \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{7} = {\\color{blue}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{2}} + ({\\color{blue}{-1}}) \\cdot ({\\color{red}{-2}}) + {\\color{blue}{3}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{1}}\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -7 \\\\ -19 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100872 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100694 :question/id #uuid "66ec04a0-2af8-5187-98b2-b06eedd5b0a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4406" :question/problem "Find $CD$, if \n![](images/1a/1a753977affc5cc3dda18c9206116e1132254bccebd1e3f2545bea75bcc38f18.png)\nand \n![](images/b9/b99016bca94adc5de11eddef950fa13fc4349aaf95387468d07f294c409d029d.png)" :question/worked-solution "Here, $C$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix and $D$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $C$ and $3$ rows in $D,$ so $C$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{3}}}{C} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\cdot} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{3}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{D} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{CD}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $CD,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-4=1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=1\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-1=2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,4=2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/64/64b831a66cdd9a3176d1eb916dfae189b41eb1f0f3ed3184797f92e404957878.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Two Matrices When They Are Conformable"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101960 :tutorial/content "It's possible to express matrix multiplication using index notation.\n\nTo see how, first let $C=AB,$ where $A$ and $B$ are two matrices that are conformable for multiplication.\n\nThen $c_{\\color{red}{i}\\color{blue}{j}}$ represents the entry at the intersection of the $\\color{red}i$th row and $\\color{blue}j$th column in $C.$\n\nTo compute $c_{\\color{red}{i}\\color{blue}{j}},$ we take the dot product of the $\\color{red}i$th row of $\\color{red}A$ and the $\\color{blue}j$th column of ${\\color{blue}B},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{i1} & {a}_{i2} & ⋯ & {a}_{in}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Index Notation of Matrix Multiplication"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100695 :question/id #uuid "3a696ed3-e341-5ea6-bf17-681ba8e386c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4407" :question/problem "Let $C=AB.$ What is the value of $c_{32}$, if\n![](images/c6/c6c7e580cfd96bc23c55742535ceb40a94635c2a44b911e0d08311c3a599c2fa.png)" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying the $3$rd row in $A$ by the $2$nd column in $B$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Entry of the Product of Two Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100741 :question/id #uuid "c3ed8d05-758d-5ae0-bf57-3779fde6020b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5885" :question/problem "Compute the product \n $\\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n       -1 \\\n \t0 \\\n        -2\n \t\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        2 & -5\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, the first factor is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1 \\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} {\\color{green}\\checkmark} {\\color{green}\\fbox{1}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} = \n\t\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign {\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n       -1 \\\n \t0 \\\n        -2\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        2 & -5\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n\n\t&= \n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        (-1)\\cdot 2 & (-1)\\cdot (-5) \\\n        0\\cdot 2 & 0\\cdot (-5)\\\n        (-2)\\cdot 2 & (-2)\\cdot (-5)\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack \\[5pt]\n\n\t&=\n\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n       -2 & 5 \\\n       0 &  0 \\\n       -4 &  10 \n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack \n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Column-Vector By a Row-Vector"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101680}}] :activity/title "Multiplying Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101055}] [#edb/ref 17592186048783 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101674} :activity/id #uuid "e035a7b5-8ab1-5910-8886-36efa38ee822" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101958 :tutorial/content "Given two matrices, it is not always possible to multiply them together. To demonstrate why, consider the following two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 12 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNormally, to compute the product $AB,$ we multiply each row of $A$ by each column of $B.$ However, in this case, each row of $A$ has $\\color{blue}2$ entries, while each column of $B$ has $\\color{red}3$ entries. The number of entries do not match!\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 12 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we cannot compute the dot product, and therefore the product $AB$ is not defined." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100869 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100691 :question/id #uuid "da92ef6c-cb0b-5a4c-9a0d-0f0a9db19616" :question/math-academy-id "e-4403" :question/problem "Let\n![](images/53/5357fcb0ec150699b4d50cbd6fa9651c421ac3500660703396146362160328a2.png)\nand\n![](images/63/63d354773d145f05e73e808efe2a21164cb28d6ff19ae104d6bbde6510cf10e6.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The product $A\\times B$ is well defined\n\n- The product $B\\times A$ is well defined" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, and $B$ is a $2 \\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $2$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{2 \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{2}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{2}} \\times 3}{B} = \n\t{\\color{green}\\text{well defined}}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $B$ and $2$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\underset{2 \\times \\color{red}{3}}{B} \\underset{\\color{red}\\neq}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{red}2} \\times 2}{A} = {\\color{red}\\text{undefined}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, while statement II is false."} :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100870 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100692 :question/id #uuid "c677ee1a-d3c2-534c-985c-efaa5dc04c86" :question/math-academy-id "e-4404" :question/problem "Suppose $A$ is a $3 \\times 4$ matrix and $B$ is a $4 \\times 2$ matrix. What are the dimensions of $AB$?" :question/worked-solution "There are $4$ columns in $A$ and $4$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ equals the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication, and $AB$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{4}}}{A} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{4}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{B} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{3} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{AB}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Product of Two Matrices Given the Dimensions of the Factors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100871 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100693 :question/id #uuid "9352349d-64e0-5437-9d72-7e7f7e571064" :question/math-academy-id "e-4405" :question/problem "Let\n![](images/03/035755bae569c8c9323112891f959076dd727d3ef40ba66c6c40dadf5ddc8f84.png)\nand\n![](images/e5/e53c9ba3bb64b81e44f8f4f49ac77408b7db6ee458be3d43b687d9ed8897ad59.png)\nWhat are the dimensions of $BA?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that $A$ is a $4 \\times 2$ matrix and $B$ is a $2 \\times 4$ matrix. \n\nThere are $4$ columns in $B$ and $4$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $B$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication. \n\nThe product $BA$ has dimensions $2 \\times 2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\color{green}{\\fbox{4}}}{B} \\underset{\\color{green}\\checkmark}{\\times} \\underset{{\\color{green}\\fbox{4}} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{A} = \n\t\\underset{\\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2} \\times \\bbox[lightgray, 2pt]{2}}{BA}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Product of Two Matrices"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101985 :tutorial/content "We can only multiply two matrices if they are **conformable** for multiplication, which means that the number of columns in the first matrix must equal the number of rows in the second matrix. In other words, we can only multiply two matrices if the first matrix has dimensions $n$ and the second matrix has dimensions $n$\n\nIf the matrices $A$ and $B$ (in that order) are conformable for multiplication and have dimensions $n$ and $n$ respectively, then the product $AB$ will have the dimensions $m \\times k.$\n\nLet's illustrate these ideas with some concrete examples." :tutorial/title "Conformability for Matrix Multiplication"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101675}}] :activity/title "Conformability for Matrix Multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101057}] [#edb/ref 17592186048707 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125612} :activity/id #uuid "23c64a39-d7c6-5e7e-a49e-62d8fec54931" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125866 :tutorial/content "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function of two variables. The **partial derivative of** $\\boldsymbol{f}$ **with respect to** $\\boldsymbol x$ is computed by taking the derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ using the usual methods, *while treating $y$ as a constant.*\n\nThe partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ has the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}\n$$\n\nThis partial derivative is defined as the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare this to the derivative of a single-variable function $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also define the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial y}\t\t = \\lim_{h \\to 0}\\left(\\dfrac {f (x ,y + h) - f(x, y)} {h}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we'd differentiate $f$ with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant.\n\nWe also have the following notations for partial derivatives:\n\n$$\nf_x =  \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}, \\qquad  f_y = \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial y}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125617}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124639 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124197 :question/id #uuid "35a664b8-a895-5c52-a92f-b62c75b44744" :question/math-academy-id "e-11026" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ given that $f(x,y) = 3x\\ln y + 2ye^x.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =3x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+2{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & =3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}+2{e}^{x}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{y}+2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Two-Variable Functions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124643 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124201 :question/id #uuid "a14d53ae-1922-531d-bb2a-bda9b4829f6e" :question/math-academy-id "e-11045" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y) = y^2\\ln{x},$ express $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ as the limit of a difference quotient." :question/worked-solution "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\left(\\dfrac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}\\right).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x) = y^2 \\ln{x},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y) = y^2\\ln(x+h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim_{h \\to 0}\\left( \\dfrac{y^2\\ln(x+h) -y^2\\ln{x} }{h}\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Partial Derivatives as Limits of Difference Quotients"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125867 :tutorial/content "Consider the multivariable function $f(x,y),$ given by\n\n$$\nf (x, y)= x y^2 + e^{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ we \"fix\" the variable $y$ (in other words, treat $y$ as if it were a constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (1)+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the term $e^{y}$ vanished when taking the derivative with respect to $x.$ This is because $e^y$ is a function of $y$ only, and since we're treating $y$ as a constant, this entire term is treated as a constant and therefore differentiates to zero (just as a constant would).\n\nSimilarly, to compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ we fix the variable $x$ and then take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (2y)+({e}^{y}) \\\\  & =2xy+{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we wish to evaluate a partial derivative at a point $(x_0, y_0),$ we can use any of the following notations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f(x_0,y_0)} {\\partial x} =  \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}\\Bigg|_{(x_0,y_0)} = f_x(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nThe notations for $f_y(x_0,y_0)$ follow the same patterns." :tutorial/title "Computing Partial Derivatives"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124644 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124202 :question/id #uuid "ee2294e1-1e9a-5be5-be47-4c2e295b640a" :question/math-academy-id "e-11049" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y) = x^2y^2+\\sin{x}\\cos{y},$ evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants:\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,2\\pi )} & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot (2\\pi )^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot 4{\\pi }^{2}+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8{\\pi }^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Partial Derivative at a Point"}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Partial Derivatives" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124722}]}
